WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:04.060
Remember when everyone went nuts about the
bear attack scene in The Revenant?

00:00:04.060 --> 00:00:09.460
Even though everything looked shockingly real,
it turns out the CGI bear, and Leo, were just

00:00:09.460 --> 00:00:10.890
really good actors.

00:00:10.890 --> 00:00:15.460
Unlike that scene, however, many other seemingly
unreal movie moments were actually done with

00:00:15.460 --> 00:00:19.660
practical effects in very real, sometimes
death-defying, conditions.

00:00:19.660 --> 00:00:23.890
Here are some movie scenes you thought were
fake but were actually real.

00:00:23.890 --> 00:00:25.259
Hanging from the hood

00:00:25.259 --> 00:00:27.929
Zoe Bell is an accomplished stunt performer.

00:00:27.929 --> 00:00:32.189
She caught Quentin Tarantino's attention when
she worked as Uma Thurman's double in Kill

00:00:32.189 --> 00:00:35.620
Bill, so the director cast her in 2007's Death
Proof.

00:00:35.620 --> 00:00:40.120
Bell spent the better part of six weeks amazingly
not dying while hanging onto the hood of a

00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:44.109
Dodge Challenger while speeding around desolate,
Eastern European roads.

00:00:44.109 --> 00:00:48.199
The result is a harrowing sequence in which
she clings to the Challenger’s hood as Kurt

00:00:48.199 --> 00:00:50.910
Russell slams his car into hers again and
again.

00:00:50.910 --> 00:00:55.239
"By the end of it, I mean we were clickin
at 80 or 90 miles an hour at one point.

00:00:55.240 --> 00:00:57.220
Miles, I mean that's fast."

00:00:57.400 --> 00:01:01.980
It would have been far too ironic to die making
a film called Death Proof anyhow.

00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:03.390
The wingsuit

00:01:03.390 --> 00:01:08.250
A remake of Point Break was never really necessary,
and especially not one that got rid of the

00:01:08.250 --> 00:01:10.229
film’s original surfers for, well…

00:01:10.229 --> 00:01:16.909
"I believe that like me, the people behind
these robberies are extreme athletes."

00:01:16.909 --> 00:01:20.820
Some of the world's top extreme athletes were
invited to do their own stunts.

00:01:20.820 --> 00:01:25.540
The wingsuit scene, in particular, was shot
with four athletes who did 60 jumps, all while

00:01:25.540 --> 00:01:30.969
going faster than 145 miles per hour, according
to the film’s behind-the-scenes featurette.

00:01:30.969 --> 00:01:35.270
"The first time I watched those 5 guys enter
that crack, and I literally am like 'Wow,

00:01:35.270 --> 00:01:39.710
we are now watching one of the greatest stunts
that's ever been pulled off by human beings."

00:01:39.710 --> 00:01:44.060
Next time, it might not be worth risking their
lives for a remake of a movie with stunning

00:01:44.060 --> 00:01:45.090
dialogue like this...

00:01:45.090 --> 00:01:48.909
"I was taking shrapnel in Khe Sanh, while
you were crapping in your hands and rubbing

00:01:48.909 --> 00:01:50.450
it on your face!"

00:01:50.450 --> 00:01:52.320
The ol' road poles

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:58.240
Almost every action sequence in 2015's Mad
Max: Fury Road was created with human stunt

00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:03.290
performers, culminating in one insane final
battle featuring weapon-firing bad guys riding

00:02:03.290 --> 00:02:06.350
giant poles mounted onto speeding vehicles.

00:02:06.350 --> 00:02:10.629
Stunt coordinator Guy Norris told Rolling
Stone that a Cirque du Soleil performer trained

00:02:10.629 --> 00:02:14.590
the stuntmen in "Chinese pole work" during
an eight-week course.

00:02:14.590 --> 00:02:18.980
That's an interesting way to phrase it: The
actors were repeatedly smashed into the ground

00:02:18.980 --> 00:02:21.410
and run over by giant speeding tires.

00:02:21.410 --> 00:02:25.380
The entire movie is an astonishing adrenaline
rush, and the fact that this scene was pulled

00:02:25.380 --> 00:02:30.060
off by real people, who weren’t killed in
the process, just makes it even more incredible.

00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:31.970
Bane’s escape

00:02:31.970 --> 00:02:36.620
Christopher Nolan's love of practical effects
is no secret, so it's no surprise the director

00:02:36.620 --> 00:02:40.640
ramped up the real-life action for 2012's
The Dark Knight Rises.

00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:44.780
"Now what's the next step of your master plan?"

00:02:44.780 --> 00:02:46.170
"Crashing this plane!"

00:02:46.170 --> 00:02:50.140
In the opening scene, the villainous Bane
blows a plane in half and escapes the wreck

00:02:50.140 --> 00:02:54.260
by being pulled onto another plane by henchmen
dangling from ropes.

00:02:54.260 --> 00:02:55.550
No big deal, right?

00:02:55.550 --> 00:02:59.760
When discussing the scene at the Tribeca Film
Festival, Nolan revealed they dropped a real

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:01.400
plane out of the sky.

00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:05.930
That means real people dropped a real plane
out of the sky for a movie about a guy who

00:03:05.930 --> 00:03:07.670
dresses like a bat.

00:03:07.670 --> 00:03:09.400
Seems perfectly reasonable.

00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:11.489
Speaking of insane plane stunts...

00:03:11.489 --> 00:03:12.700
That's one way to hitchhike

00:03:12.700 --> 00:03:17.840
It’s well-known that Tom Cruise does much
of his own stunt work in the Mission Impossible

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:21.050
series, but some of his exploits defy belief.

00:03:21.050 --> 00:03:26.870
Case in point: 2015's Rogue Nation, where
Cruise pulls off at least one irresponsibly

00:03:26.870 --> 00:03:31.730
crazy maneuver: hanging from the side of a
massive cargo plane during takeoff.

00:03:31.730 --> 00:03:35.310
While the dangers involved with this stunt
are obvious, Cruise could have been killed

00:03:35.310 --> 00:03:37.430
if so much as a random bird flew into him.

00:03:37.430 --> 00:03:39.849
"I mean, but you were getting hit with jet
fuel and-"

00:03:39.849 --> 00:03:44.510
"Jet fuel and, ya know I just didn't wanna
get hit by a bird or any particle, ya know-"

00:03:44.510 --> 00:03:45.760
"No seriously that-"

00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:49.750
"No seriously I got hit by a little stone
about that big and we were going so fast,

00:03:49.750 --> 00:03:50.850
luckily it hit my rib.

00:03:50.850 --> 00:03:52.450
I thought I broke my rib."

00:03:52.450 --> 00:03:54.790
So they had to do it in one take, right?

00:03:54.790 --> 00:03:55.840
Think again.

00:03:55.840 --> 00:03:58.340
Cruise did it eight times.

00:03:58.340 --> 00:04:02.270
There's dedication to a role, and then there's
whatever you call what Tom Cruise does.

00:04:02.270 --> 00:04:04.780
"Ha ha ha ha ha ha!"

00:04:04.780 --> 00:04:05.829
Thanks for watching!

00:04:05.829 --> 00:04:09.860
Subscribe to our YouTube channel to watch
more videos like the one you just saw.

00:04:09.860 --> 00:04:12.999
And leave us a comment to let us know about
your favorite movie stunts...

